From c9747824e3dce7e563c6174c40354d71e0473066 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jjuvonen-amd <joni.juvonen@amd.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 12:54:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [ROCm] symm_mem: fused all-gather + matmul and matmul +
 reduce-scatter on SDMA engines (prototype)

For large inputs, fused_all_gather_matmul and fused_matmul_reduce_scatter copy
shards out of peers' symmetric-memory buffers with tensor copies. On ROCm, HIP
runs concurrent copies from those buffers one at a time, so the all-gather
moves at about one link's rate, and the copies compete with the matmuls for
compute units.

This adds an opt-in ROCm path (TORCH_SYMM_MEM_ENABLE_ROCM_SDMA=1). Every rank
pushes its data into each peer's symmetric-memory inbox with ROCr's
hsa_amd_memory_async_copy_on_engine: one SDMA engine per peer (the engine ROCr
prefers for writing to that peer), all peers at once, in pieces, each piece's
matmul starting as soon as it has landed. A spare engine writes a per-sender
flag once a peer's piece has landed; a one-thread kernel waits for the flags,
bounded by a timeout, and another releases the copies after the
symmetric-memory barrier. Rows land in natural order, so results, return_A and
output strides match the default path.

Captured calls issue their copies from a host-function node on every replay.
Unsupported cases (last-dim gather/scatter, the fp8 scaled ops, unvalidated
architectures unless TORCH_SYMM_MEM_ROCM_SDMA_ALLOW_UNTESTED=1) take the
default path.

The transport (_rocm_sdma.hip) is built with torch.utils.cpp_extension on
first use and called through ctypes; a C++ port under
torch/csrc/distributed/c10d/symm_mem is the follow-up for review.

Test plan:
python test/distributed/test_symmetric_memory.py -k "AsyncTPTest or AsyncTPRocmSdmaTest"
TORCH_SYMM_MEM_ENABLE_ROCM_SDMA=1 python test/distributed/test_symmetric_memory.py -k AsyncTPTest
